  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Monday)\nIssued at 1136 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe pattern will remain stagnant over the next week with a sprawling \nand strong upper level high either overhead or nearby. Temperatures \nwill remain consistent, with highs in the upper 90s to low 100s each \nday and lows mostly in the 70s. Dew points will remain in the low to \nmid 60s through Tuesday, so even though temperatures will be hot, \nheat indices will only run slightly higher than the temperatures. \nThis will continue to keep heat products confined to the typically \nhotter locations. For Tuesday, the Heat Advisory will span the lower \nelevations of northeast OK and west-central AR. \n\nBy Wednesday and onwards, the low level flow will become more \nsoutheasterly and strengthen slightly as a weak decaying tropical\nsystem moves into Texas, which will help low level moisture creep\nupwards. The chance for rain is essentially zero through the work\nweek for most locations. The one exception will be parts of \nsoutheast OK on Thursday, as the remnants of the tropical system \ngraze southeast OK, bringing a 10-15% chance of a few showers. \n\nA similar 5-10% chance of diurnal showers is possible for the \nhigher terrain of northwest AR next weekend as the upper level \nflow becomes northwesterly. In any case, meaningful precipitation \nremains extremely unlikely for now. With continued hot, dry, and \nbreezy weather, some fire weather potential will exist each \nafternoon throughout the region.",
